","Saltatory conduction is a type of nerve impulse transmission that is faster and more efficient than non-saltatory conduction. It occurs in nerve fibres called medulimentary fibres, which have a protective covering called the myic sheath. In contrast, non-saltatory conduction occurs in non-meulminary fibres, which lack this sheath.

In saltatory conduction, the nerve impulse travels rapidly through the gaps between the myymy sheath called nodes of Zannolli. This is because the myymy sheath acts as an insulator, allowing the electrical current to flow through the nerve fibre without significant resistance. As a result, the nerve impulse can be transmitted much more efficiently and at a much faster rate than in non-saltatory conduction.

In non-saltatory conduction, the nerve impulse travels along the entire length of the nerve fibre, with the electrical current flowing through the entire cell wall. This process is less efficient and slower, as the electrical current encounters resistance along the entire length of the nerve fibre.

In summary, saltatory conduction is a more efficient and faster type of nerve impulse transmission that occurs in medulimentary fibres with the presence of the myymy sheath. Non-saltatory conduction, on the other hand, is less efficient and slower, occurring in non-meulminary fibres without the myymy sheath.
