</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the correct dose of the drug, we first need to convert the patient's weight and height to meters and kilograms.

1. **Convert weight to kilograms:**
   \[
   65 \text{ lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2045 \text{ lb}} = 33.58 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. **Convert height to meters:**
   \[
   3 \text{ ft} \times 30.48 \text{ cm/ft} + 6 \text{ in} \times 2.54 \text{ cm/in} = 9.12 \text{ cm} + 15.24 \text{ cm} = 10.36 \text{ cm}
   \]
   \[
   10.36 \text{ cm} \times \frac{1 \text{ m}}{100 \text{ cm}} = 0.1036 \text{ m}
   \]

3. **Calculate the body surface area (BSA) using the Duvadana formula:**
   \[
   \text{BSA} = \sqrt{\frac{77 \times \text{kg} \times \text{m}}{14.3}} = \sqrt{\frac{77 \times 33.58 \times 0.1036}{14.3}} = 0.1036 \text{ m}
   \]

4. **Calculate the dose:**
   \[
   17.5 \text{ mg/m}^2 \times 0.1036 \text{ m}^2 = 1.82 \text{ mg}
   \]

Thus, the correct dose of the drug for this patient is \(\boxed{1.82 \text{ mg}}\).

