What is the effect of stimulation of arterial baroreceptors on the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors activates neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). However, most of these NTS neurons exhibit weak or no pulse rhythmicity in their activity. This observation is puzzling because:  
1. Arterial baroreceptors with myelinated axons show strong pulse rhythmicity.  
2. Neurons downstream in the baroreceptor reflex pathway (e.g., in the CVLM, RVLM, and sympathetic pre- and postganglionic neurons) exhibit strong pulse rhythmicity.  
3. Even minimal pulsatile stimulation of intact baroreceptor afferents can induce cardiac rhythmicity in sympathetic vasoconstrictor neurons.  

The traditional explanation for this discrepancy is the high degree of convergence of baroreceptor afferents onto NTS neurons and further convergence of NTS outputs onto neurons in the CVLM. However, this model may not fully account for the observed experimental results.  

Thus, while NTS neurons are activated by baroreceptor stimulation, their activity lacks the strong rhythmicity seen in other parts of the reflex pathway.  

\boxed{\text{Most NTS neurons activated by arterial baroreceptor stimulation show weak or no pulse rhythmicity in their activity.}}
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractritus solitulus (NTS). This occurs because arterial baroreceptors are specialized receptors that detect changes in blood pressure. When blood pressure increases, these receptors stretch and release a greater number of acetylcholine molecules, which are then released into the sympathetic nervous system. This release of acetylcholine stimulates the neurons in the NTS, which in turn leads to a decrease in blood pressure. This negative feedback mechanism helps to maintain blood pressure within a narrow range.

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

