<USER_REQUEST>
What is the role of ARTEMIS in DNA repair during V(D)J recombination, and why is this important for immune system function?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
ARTEMIS is a protein essential for DNA repair, particularly for repairing double-stranded DNA breaks generated during V(D)J recombination. V(D)J recombination is a critical process in the development of T and B lymphocytes, as it enables the generation of diverse antigen receptors by rearranging variable (V), diversity (D), and joining (J) gene segments. ARTEMIS facilitates the repair of these DNA breaks, ensuring proper immune cell development.  

If ARTEMIS is mutated, DNA breaks during V(D)J recombination remain unrepaired, leading to arrested T- and B-cell development. This results in a rare form of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) called T–B–SCID, where patients lack functional T and B cells, leaving them highly susceptible to infections.  

Thus, ARTEMIS plays a vital role in immune system function by ensuring the successful completion of V(D)J recombination.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

