Given that the standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol and we are using 2.5 moles of propane, we can plug these values into the equation:

q = (2.5 moles) × (-2220 kJ/mol)

q = -5550 kJ

The negative sign indicates that the reaction is exothermic, meaning that heat is released. Therefore, 5550 kJ of heat is released when 2.5 moles of propane are completely burned in oxygen gas.
