The standard enthalpy change for this reaction (ΔH°) can be calculated using the following equation:

ΔH° = ΔHf(FeCl4]2- - [ΔHf(Fe(H2O)6]2+ + 2ΔHf(Cl-)]

where ΔHf represents the enthalpy of formation for each species.

We are given the following values:

ΔHf([FeCl4]2-) = -392 kJ/mol
ΔHf(Fe2+) = -340 kJ/mol (hydration enthalpy of Fe2+)
ΔHf(Cl-) = -381 kJ/mol (hydration enthalpy of Cl-)

First, we need to find the enthalpy of formation for [Fe(H2O)6]2+. We can do this by using the hydration enthalpy of Fe2+ and the enthalpy of formation of the complex ion:

ΔHf([Fe(H2O)6]2+) = ΔHf(Fe2+) + 6ΔHf(H2O)

Since the enthalpy of formation of water (H2O) is -285.8 kJ/mol, we can calculate the enthalpy of formation for [Fe(H2O)6]2+:

ΔHf([Fe(H2O)6]2+) = -340 kJ/mol + 6(-285.8 kJ/mol) = -1383.2 kJ/mol

Now we can calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction:

ΔH° = -392 kJ/mol - (-1383.2 kJ/mol) - 2(-381 kJ/mol)
ΔH° = -392 kJ/mol + 1383.2 kJ/mol + 762 kJ/mol
ΔH° = 1813.2 kJ/mol

