","To test the differentiability at \( x = 0 \), we analyze the limit of the derivative at \( x = 0 \):
\[
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f(x) - f(0)}{x - 0} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{x \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x} = \lim_{x \to 0} \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right}.
\]
The limit of \( \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x} \right) \) as \( x \to 0 \) is \( \frac{\pi}{2} \). Thus, the derivative exists at \( x = 0 \), and \( f(x) \) is differentiable at \( x = 0 \). 

The derivative at \( x = 0 \) is:
\[
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f(x) - f(0)}{x - 0} = \frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

Final Answer: The function \( f(x) \) is differentiable at \( x = 0 \), and its derivative at \( x = 0 \) is \( \boxed{\frac{\pi}{2}} \).
