1. **Understanding the Terms**:
   - \(L_{\alpha}\) is the \(\alpha\)-th ordinal in the **L-series** (Listing of the universe).
   - \(V_{\alpha}\) is the \(\alpha\)-th star of the **V-set** (V is the set of all sets).
   - \(\beta_{\alpha}\) is the \(\alpha\)-th Binet number (a measure of the number of functions from \(\mathbb{N}\) to \(\mathbb{N}\)).

2. **Why the Statement is Not True**:
   - For \(\alpha = \omega\), \(L_{\omega} = V_{\omega}\) holds, but \(\omega \neq \beta_{\omega}\).
   - For \(\alpha = \beta_{\omega}\), \(L_{\beta_{\omega}} = V_{\beta_{\omega}}\) holds, but \(\beta_{\omega} \neq \beta_{\beta_{\omega}}\).

3. **Key Observations**:
   - The L-series is defined as the set of all functions defined on \(\mathbb{N}\) with values in \(\mathbb{N}\), while the V-set is defined as the set of all sets.
   - The Bint numbers are defined as the number of functions from \(\mathbb{N}\) to \(\mathbb{N}\), which is a different notion of size.
   - The equality \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right\}\) is not necessarily equivalent to \(\alpha = \beta_{\alpha}\).

Thus, the statement is not true, as the equality \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right\}\) does not hold if and only if \(\alpha = \beta_{\alpha}\). 

\boxed{\text{The statement is not true.}}
