Prove that the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, but they do commute if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions in the expressions for \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) and \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) vanish. What are these conditions explicitly?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To prove the non-commutativity of the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \), we analyze the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \). The expressions for these derivatives are as follows:

\[
aD_t^p f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{p-1} \frac{d}{dt} \left[ \int_0^t (t - s)^{p-1} f(s) \, ds \right] \, dt,
\]

\[
aD_t^q f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{q-1} \frac{d}{dt} \left[ \int_0^t (t - s)^{q-1} f(s) \, ds \right] \, dt.
\]

Substituting these into the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) and simplifying yields a double integral with non-trivial terms involving \( f(t) \) and its derivatives. In general, this difference is non-zero, confirming that \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute.

However, if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions vanish, the difference simplifies to zero. These conditions are:

1. The integral of \( f(t) \) from zero to \( t \) vanishes, i.e., \( \int_0^t f(s) \, ds = 0 \).
2. The integral of \( t^{p-1} f(t) \) from zero to \( t \) vanishes, i.e., \( \int_0^t t^{p-1} f(t) \, dt = 0 \).
3. The integral of \( t^{q-1} f(t) \) from zero to \( t \) vanishes, i.e., \( \int_0^t t^{q-1} f(t) \, dt = 0 \).

These conditions ensure that the terms involving the lower terminal conditions cancel out, making \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) commute. 

Thus, the commutativity of the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators depends on the specific function \( f(t) \) and the values of \( p \) and \( q \). 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{No commutativity unless:} \\
&\int_0^t f(s) \, ds = 0, \quad \int_0^t t^{p-1} f(t) \, dt = 0, \quad \int_0^t t^{q-1} f(t) &= 0.
\end{aligned}
}
