- \( n \) is the number of turns in the coil,
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil,
- \( a \) is the radius of the coil.

Given the values:
- \( n = 100 \)
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( a = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

Substituting these values into the formula:
\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A) (100) (1 \, \text{A})}{2 (0.2 \, \text{m})}
\]
\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T·m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B \approx 3.14 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the circular coil is \(\boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}}\).
