\]  
where \(\Delta E\) is the uncertainty in energy, \(\Delta t\) is the uncertainty in time, and \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi} = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\)).  

1. **Calculate the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\))**:  
   \[
   \Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2 \times 1.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
   \]  
   Convert this to MeV:  
   \[
   \Delta E = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} = 32.6 \text{ MeV}
   \]  

2. **Determine the range of outcomes**:  
   The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{1385 \pm 32.6 \text{ MeV}}\).  

This means that the experimentally observed rest energy of the Σ⁺ particle will typically fall within this range due to the uncertainty principle.
