<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The proton's acceleration is due to the electric field, which exerts a force on the proton. The force \(F\) on the proton is given by:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]  
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), the acceleration \(a\) is:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.78 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
(b) The time \(t\) for the proton to reach a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is found from the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
where \(u = 0\) (since the proton starts from rest).  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.78 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.75 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]  
Final answers:  
(a) The magnitude of the proton's acceleration is \(\boxed{4.78 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time it takes the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(\boxed{6.75 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}\).
